module sh.sysl.sha3.c
@tests

// What the raw layer claims, checked against FIPS 202 and the C ABI rather than against the header.
//
// **These are the survey's probes, kept.** The module above places the sponge's state inside an
// ordinary sysl struct, as an array of `u64`, and two facts have to hold for that to be sound rather
// than merely working today. Both are asserted here, because neither is visible from the pleasant
// layer and a change in either would corrupt digests rather than fail to compile.

// **The load-bearing one.** `[N]u64` storage is 8-aligned by construction, and that is the *only*
// thing making the state's address good enough for `sha3_ctx_t`. If the C type ever wanted more --
// a vector-unit rewrite reading the lanes 16 bytes at a time would -- the array would be under-aligned
// and every digest wrong, so this is the assertion that would catch it.
//
// Eight is not arbitrary: the state is `uint64_t q[25]`, and a 64-bit lane wants a 64-bit boundary.
@test("the sponge state is 8-aligned, which is what a u64 array supplies")
state_wants_eight_byte_alignment()
    assert_eq(CTX_ALIGN, 8)

// FIPS 202 fixes Keccak's state at 1600 bits, so the context cannot be smaller than the 200 bytes
// that holds it -- whatever bookkeeping upstream puts after it.
@test("the context holds a whole 1600-bit Keccak state")
context_holds_the_state()
    assert(CTX_SIZE >= 200, "a 1600-bit state is 200 bytes")

// The rounding the module above does must not lose a byte. `WORDS` there is `(CTX_SIZE + 7) / 8`, and
// this is that arithmetic checked at the only place both numbers are in scope.
@test("rounding the context up to whole lanes covers all of it")
rounding_covers_the_context()
    assert((CTX_SIZE + 7) / 8 * 8 >= CTX_SIZE, "the rounded lane count covers the context")
